 ### [Random Hemisphere Sampling](2034-random-hemisphere-sampling.md)
-Random Hemisphere Sampling generates a random unit vector uniformly distributed within a hemisphere oriented around a given normal vector. It is primarily used to compute indirect lighting contributions at a surface point by sampling the hemisphere above the surface normal and recursively tracing rays. It sets itself apart from its parent [Uniform Sphere Sampling](1669-uniform-sphere-sampling.md) by restricting the sample space to a half-sphere, which is essential for surface-based shading models where light only interacts with one side of a surface. Unlike [Rejection Sampling for Uniform Sphere Sampling](3286-rejection-sampling-for-uniform-sphere-sampling.md), which discards samples falling outside the hemisphere, this method directly constructs valid hemisphere directions, ensuring 100% efficiency per sample generation attempt.
