 ### [Rejection Sampling for Uniform Sphere Sampling](3286-rejection-sampling-for-uniform-sphere-sampling.md)
-Rejection Sampling for Uniform Sphere Sampling generates uniform sphere samples by rejecting points that fall outside the unit sphere or outside a desired region. This method is less efficient than direct sampling but can be useful when sampling from complex distributions or constrained regions where direct inversion is difficult. It sets itself apart from the direct method by potentially discarding samples, requiring a loop until a valid sample is found.
+Rejection Sampling for Uniform Sphere Sampling generates uniformly distributed points within a unit ball (or on its surface) by sampling from a bounding cube and discarding outliers [G]. It differs from spherical coordinate conversion [Uniform Sphere Sampling](1669-uniform-sphere-sampling.md) by avoiding trigonometric functions, relying instead on simple arithmetic and normalization, which makes it computationally cheaper per sample but potentially slower in wall-clock time due to rejected iterations [G]. It sets itself apart from the direct method by potentially discarding samples, requiring a loop until a valid sample is found.
 
 ### [Spherical Background Sampling](3991-spherical-background-sampling.md)
-Spherical Background Sampling computes spherical coordinates (theta, phi) on a background sphere for rays, assuming ray origins are inside the sphere. It is used for skybox or environment map sampling. It sets itself apart by focusing on the background geometry rather than general direction generation, often integrating with camera models to determine the ray direction towards the background.
